course: dictionary
lesson: 13888
title: kW and kWh: power and energy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: kW is short for kilowatt, a unit of power. One kilowatt is 1,000 watts. Power tells you how fast a machine uses energy. «This kettle is 3 kW» means it uses 3,000 watts while it is working.
explain: Look at the clues around kW. Kettles, heaters and car engines are machines, so kW tells you how strong each one is. A bigger number means more power. «The heater is 2 kW, but the car engine is 90 kW.» The engine needs far more power, so its number is much bigger.
explain: kWh is different. It stands for kilowatt-hour, a unit of energy, and it is written on electricity bills. «We used 250 kWh this month.» The h stands for hour, because energy is counted over time. Power is how fast energy is used, and energy is the total amount used.
fact: kW stands for kilowatt.
fact: One kilowatt is 1,000 watts.
fact: kW measures power, which is how fast a machine uses energy.
fact: kWh stands for kilowatt-hour, a unit of energy.
fact: Electricity bills count kWh, so they show how much energy your home has used.
exercise: «The heater uses 2 kW of power.» What does kW measure? | Energy on a bill | *Power | Length | Time
exercise: «Our electricity bill shows 250 kWh.» What does kWh measure? | Power of a kettle | *Energy used over time | Weight of a heater | Speed of a car
exercise: How many watts are in one kilowatt? | 100 | *1,000 | 10 | 10,000
test: «The car engine is rated at 90 kW.» How many watts is that? | *90,000 watts | 90 watts | 900 watts | 9 watts
test: Which unit is written on an electricity bill to show energy used? | kW | *kWh | watts | volts
exam: «The kettle is 3 kW and the bill shows 12 kWh.» Which unit stands for power? | bill | kWh | *kW | hour
